WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.700
Here is a made-up neuron, resting at −71 mV.

2
00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:13.860
Potassium current leaves it all the time, about 200 pA.

3
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:19.220
Sodium current enters it all the time, also about 200 pA.

4
00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:22.060
Neither ion is at equilibrium.

5
00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:26.580
Yet the voltage does not move. Why?

7
00:00:26.633 --> 00:00:29.353
Each ion has an equilibrium potential.

8
00:00:30.033 --> 00:00:34.893
That is the voltage where the electrical force on the ion balances its concentration

9
00:00:34.893 --> 00:00:37.833
gradient. Here the values are supplied.

10
00:00:38.513 --> 00:00:42.373
For potassium, EK is −95 mV.

11
00:00:42.873 --> 00:00:46.393
For sodium, ENa is +60.6 mV.

12
00:00:47.573 --> 00:00:49.973
Driving force is the distance from equilibrium.

13
00:00:50.593 --> 00:00:55.353
For potassium it is +23.8 mV, so the current goes outward.

14
00:00:55.353 --> 00:01:00.673
For sodium it is −131.8 mV, so the current goes inward.

15
00:01:01.467 --> 00:01:04.807
Current is conductance times driving force.

16
00:01:05.927 --> 00:01:10.567
Potassium has 8.47 nS of open leak channels.

17
00:01:11.347 --> 00:01:15.067
That gives about +202 pA outward.

18
00:01:16.627 --> 00:01:22.427
Sodium has only 1.53 nS. But its driving force is much larger.

19
00:01:23.147 --> 00:01:26.287
It gives −202 pA inward.

20
00:01:27.187 --> 00:01:33.627
The two cancel. Rest means zero net current, not zero current for each ion.

21
00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:38.780
Left alone, these leaks would run the gradients down.

22
00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:46.140
The sodium-potassium pump puts the ions back, three sodium out and two potassium

23
00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.960
in. Its main job is keeping the gradients.

24
00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:54.100
So rest is a steady state held up by the pump.

25
00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.180
It is not an equilibrium.

26
00:01:57.183 --> 00:02:03.143
The Goldman-Hodgkin-Katz equation finds the voltage where total current is zero.

27
00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:07.663
Each ion counts in proportion to its relative permeability.

28
00:02:08.523 --> 00:02:14.103
In this made-up neuron, potassium is 1 and sodium is 0.04.

29
00:02:14.943 --> 00:02:18.263
The result is −71.2 mV.

30
00:02:18.763 --> 00:02:22.163
That sits 24 mV positive of EK.

31
00:02:22.163 --> 00:02:25.143
The small sodium leak holds it there.

32
00:02:26.117 --> 00:02:30.837
Now double the sodium permeability, to 0.08.

33
00:02:31.577 --> 00:02:38.737
The balance point moves to −58.9 mV. That is 12 mV toward ENa.

34
00:02:39.697 --> 00:02:44.937
Raising one ion's permeability moves the voltage toward that ion's equilibrium

35
00:02:44.937 --> 00:02:50.637
potential. Other cells have other ratios, so there is no universal resting

36
00:02:50.637 --> 00:02:52.917
potential. Keep this rule.

37
00:02:53.897 --> 00:02:57.717
Rest is where the leaks cancel, and permeability sets where.
